In "The Prometheus Syndrom" by Bettina Liebowitz Knapp, published 1979
by Whitston Publishing Company, the author suggests the existence of a
previously unsuspected syndrome which she names Prometheus.

The Prometheus syndrome involves fascination with three things: flight,
fire, and urine.  The latter element often manifests itself as
bedwetting.  The author examines case histories over several centuries
in which these three elements are found.

Extropian philosophy is quite Promethean in nature.  The Extropian
motto of "Forward, Outward, Upward," suggests flight.  Many list
members are interested in air and space travel and a considerable
number are pilots, skydivers, and so on.

List members have also described youthful experimentation with
pyrotechnics and other forms of fire.  Fire plays a large symbolic part
in Ayn Rand's writings, representing the force of man's reason, and
Objectivism has had major influences on Extropianism.

Is it too much to suspect that the third element of Knapp's syndrome is
an equally prevalent, although for obvious reasons less discussed,
element of Extropian thought?

Something to think about, from...

The Anti-Klaus!

