Date: Fri, 8 Oct 1993 13:52:09 -0700
From: dkrieger@Synopsys.COM (Dave Krieger)
Subject: CRYP: Apple System 7 Pro

Folks --

I just had a peek at Apple's forthcoming System 7 Pro, which has mail
capabilities and licensed RSA document signature capability (no encryption,
for the usual bullshit export reasons).

Apple is getting into the central key authentication business -- you
register your public key with them.  However, the way in which public keys
get distributed among users is very wacky.  There is no "public key"
document that you can pass around via verifiable channels to those with
whom you wish to correspond.  Instead, apparently, your public key is sent
with each signed message, encrypted with a private key held by Apple.  Your
public key is then decrypted with Apple's public key (built into the
software, apparently) at the receiver end.

Let the notation Xp(T) mean the action of user X's public key on the
message T, and Xs(T) mean the action of X's secret key on T.  For RSA
encryption, Xp(Xs(T)) = T, and Xs(Xp(T)) = T (each key undoes the action of
the other).  Let U be you and A be Apple.

When you register your public key with Apple (they don't show you the
actual public key you've generated; it's hidden in an electronic
"registration form" you send to Apple on disk), they send you the
Apple-signed public key that will be included with all your signed
messages, As(Up).  

When you send a signed message to a colleague, his system software already
includes Apple's public key, so to verify your signature, his system does
Ap(As(Up) = Up to get your public key, then verifies your signature with
that.

This prevents you from using a non-Apple-licensed key with the system;
without Apple's signature on your public key, your signature fails to
verify.

This has probably already been on cypherpunks; apologies to those who've
seen it before.
