Actually, the name Cohen comes from the Kohanim, who were the class from 
which priests were recruited in ancient Israel.  The other two classes 
were the temple servant Levites (whence my name) and the Israelites, 
meaning everybody else.  There were twelve tribes, which were not the 
same as classes -- though there was a tribe of Levi.  Confused?  Me too.  

Tim may have a point, though.  Much Jewish humor is zen-like.  Here's my 
favorite example:

Mr. Greenberg walks into a restaurant, sits at his usual table, and 
orders his usual bowl of soup.  The waiter brings the soup and is about 
to walk away when Greenberg calls to him...

Greenberg: Waiter, There's something wrong.  I want you to taste this     
           soup. 
Waiter:    Mr. Greenberg, that's the same soup you've been ordering for
	   twenty years.  We made it fresh this morning.  I assure you,
	   it's fine.
Greenberg: No, I insist.
Waiter:    Really, Mr. Greenberg, there's no need.
Greenberg: Do you want to me call the manager?
Waiter:    Oh, all right.  Uh... where's the spoon?
Greenberg: Aha!

-- Simon!



