Subject: Meaninglessness

At 12:02 AM 10/7/93 +0000, Michael Clive Price wrote: [not it original order]
>I think Dave and I are in general agreement, but I'll try
>to round off some of the open questions:

Never in the history of the west have two people who agree on so much
argued so much about so little! An unfortunate self-criticism!

>Clearly if we demand someone to define every word they use, recursively,
>they lost in an infinite regress and the discussion becomes stymied. 
>True, but I don't think Perry was doing this.  The meaning of
>good/bad/evil etc is fairly central to any discussion of ethics/morals
>(or whatever we're calling it today!), so it was quite a reasonable
>question to ask.

So why is it such a clever move to stump them with this definition
question? This goes back to my original question on meaninglessness. There
is a big difference between the following two statements:

'Your concepts are not operational, therefore I'm not interested in arguing
with you.'

'Your concepts are not operational, therefore you're wrong.' (or therefore
you should just forget the whole thing.)

This relates to some slippery rhetoric often used to defend practices in
Economics. Among the tribe of Econ, it takes a theory to beat a theory. No
matter how absurd a theory or technique is, unless you've got something
better they're not going to listen to you. (I exaggerate for effect.) Of
course, the same could be asked of critics of astrology or parapsychology,
but that doesn't appear to bother the Economists.

Hence Perry's opponents are entitled to ask him what he's got to offer that
deals with their problem better than the language that they're using. These
people can use these words correctly in sentences, and can detect their
incorrect use in others' sentences. Our culture has imbued those words with
complex and subtle meaning. While the dictionary definitions may leave
something to be desired, that hardly justifies eliminating discussion of
good and evil from all conversations. You may convince me that astrologists
are charlatans, but not that discussions of good and evil have no referent.

I hope I haven't misinterpreted the whole point. As you may have guessed,
people who go around asking for definitions are a pet peeve of mine. Unless
I'm using an eclectic or jargonized form of a word, or my partner in
conversation is not fluent in English, I find this annoying. Even the
dictionary resorts often to giving examples of word usage and antonyms
rather than depending only upon a formal definition.

Wittgenstein (bzzt! bzzt! name drop alert!) said (I paraphrase) 'Don't look
for the meaning of a word, look for how it is used.' (Or was it Peirce?) We
are free to take his advice or ignore it, just as we can answer Perry's
challenge or ignore it.

>Who is Michael Polanyi, and what does he say? - I'm curious.

He a physicalchemist who turned philosopher. Hayek credits him with coinage
of the phrase 'spontaneous order.' The book I was thinking of was PERSONAL
KNOWLEDGE. From the preface:

"This is primarily an enquiry into the nature and justification of
scientific knowledge. But my reconsideration of scientific knowlege leads
on to a wide range of questions outside science.

"I start by rejecting the ideal of scientific detachment. In the exact
sciences, this false ideal is perhaps harmless, for it is in fact
disregarded there by scientists. But we shall see that it exercises a
destructive influence in biology, psychology and sociology, and falsifies
our whole outlook far beyond the domain of science. I want to establish an
alternative ideal of knowledge, quite generally."

As I recall, the subjects of inarticulate skills and tacit knowledge formed
an important part of his argument.  Something else from this book that I
mentioned on the list a few months ago concerns Polanyi's effort to
discredit the historical account of the development of relativity. He calls
'historical fiction' the story that 'relativity was conceived by Einstein
in 1905 in order to account for the negative result of the Michelson-Morley
experiment.'

